/*]]>*/</style>
<div class=3D"toc-macro rbtoc1758122854193">
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Introduction">Introduction</a=
></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Design">Design</a>
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Components">Components</a>
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-AI+Utility">AI+ Utility</a></=
li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-RabbitMQ">RabbitMQ</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Redis">Redis</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-NotificationAPI">Notification=
 API</a></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Workflow">Workflow</a>
<ul class=3D"toc-indentation">
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-AI+Common">AI+ Common</a></li=
>
</ul></li>
</ul></li>
<li><a href=3D"#AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Integrations">Integrations</a=
></li>
</ul>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Introduction">Introduction</h1>
<p>The current architecture for managing AI job status relies heavily on di=
rect HTTP callbacks from the AI+ utility to the Notification API utility. T=
his approach has proven to be unreliable, particularly in scenarios where t=
he API gateway experiences downtime or restarts. When the Notification API =
is unavailable, the AI utility's status updates fail, leading to job failur=
es and an inconsistent system state. This dependency creates a fragile syst=
em where a single point of failure can disrupt critical AI inference jobs.<=
/p>
<p>To address these limitations, this document proposes a redesigned archit=
ecture that leverages RabbitMQ for asynchronous message queuing and Redis f=
or persistent job state management. This new design aims to decouple the AI=
 utility from the Notification API, ensuring reliable status updates, impro=
ving overall system resilience, and enabling scalability and statelessness =
of the Notification API.</p>
<p>The primary issue stems from the direct dependency on HTTP callbacks for=
 status updates. If the Notification API is unavailable, the AI utility can=
not communicate its status, leading to job failures. This creates a single =
point of failure that negatively impacts system reliability. Additionally, =
the current in-memory job state management within the AI+ utility is volati=
le, lacking persistence and scalability. This limits the system's ability t=
o handle high volumes of jobs and recover from failures gracefully.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Design">Design</h1>
<p>We want something with these general tenents:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Improved Reliability:</strong> Eliminates single points of failu=
re.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Enhanced Scalability:</strong> Leverages RabbitMQ and Redis for =
high throughput.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Simplified Architecture:</strong> Removes the need for complex H=
TTP callback logic.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Centralized Job State Management:</strong> Provides a single sou=
rce of truth for job status.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Increased Flexibility:</strong> Allows for easy integration of d=
ownstream consumers and other DataCore components (Swarm Object Storage, Pi=
xit)</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Given the above, the proposed solution centers around the integration of=
 RabbitMQ and Redis to create a robust and scalable job status management s=
ystem.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Components"><strong>Components</str=
ong></h2>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-AI+Utility"><strong>AI+ Utility </s=
trong></h3>
<p>This component initiates AI inference jobs, writes job state to Redis, a=
nd publishes status updates to RabbitMQ. It is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Receiving job requests and initiating the AI inference process.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Generating a unique <code>job_id</code> for each job.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Writing the initial job state to Redis, including information like <code=
>job_id</code>, <code>media_file</code>, <code>status</code>, and <code>tim=
estamp</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Publishing status update messages to RabbitMQ whenever the job status ch=
anges. These messages include the <code>job_id</code>, the new <code>status=
</code>, and any other relevant information.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Updating the job status in Redis as the job progresses (e.g., "processin=
g," "completed," "failed").</p></li>
<li>
<p>Ensuring atomicity of updates to Redis and publishing messages to Rabbit=
MQ to maintain data consistency.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-RabbitMQ"><strong>RabbitMQ</strong>=
</h3>
<p>This message queue acts as a reliable intermediary for status updates. I=
t is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Receiving status update messages from the AI Utility.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Routing messages to the correct queue based on routing keys or exchange =
configurations.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Providing persistence and delivery guarantees to ensure that messages ar=
e not lost.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Enabling asynchronous communication between the AI Utility and the API G=
ateway.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Redis"><strong>Redis </strong></h3>
<p>This in-memory data store provides persistent storage for job state. It =
is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Storing job details such as <code>job_id</code>, <code>media_file</code>=
, <code>status</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, and other relevant informati=
on.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Providing fast read and write access to job state data.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Offering data persistence through mechanisms like RDB or AOF files.</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p>Enabling efficient management of job state data using data structures li=
ke hashes and sets.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-NotificationAPI"><strong>Notificati=
on API </strong></h3>
<p>This component consumes status updates from RabbitMQ and sends notificat=
ions to upstream consumers. It is responsible for:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Subscribing to the appropriate RabbitMQ queue to receive status update m=
essages.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Reading the current job state from Redis using the <code>job_id</code> i=
ncluded in the message.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Determining the appropriate downstream consumers to notify based on the =
job status and other relevant information.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Sending notifications to downstream consumers (e.g., via webhooks)</p></=
li>
<li>
<p>Acknowledging processed messages in RabbitMQ to prevent redelivery.</p><=
/li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Workflow">Workflow</h2>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>The AI+ Utility receives a job request and initiates the inference proce=
ss.</p></li>
<li>
<p>The AI+ Utility generates a unique <code>job_id</code> and writes the in=
itial job state to Redis, including information like <code>job_id</code>, <=
code>media_file</code>, <code>status</code>, and <code>timestamp</code>.</p=
></li>
<li>
<p>The AI+ Utility publishes a status update message to a designated Rabbit=
MQ exchange. This message includes the <code>job_id</code>, <code>status</c=
ode>, and any other relevant information.</p></li>
<li>
<p>RabbitMQ routes the message to a dedicated queue.</p></li>
<li>
<p>The Notification API, acting as a consumer, retrieves messages from the =
RabbitMQ queue.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Upon receiving a status update, the API Gateway reads the current job st=
ate from Redis using the <code>job_id</code>.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Based on the job status and any other relevant information, the Notifica=
tion API sends notifications to downstream consumers (e.g., via webhooks)</=
p></li>
<li>
<p>The Notification API acknowledges the message in RabbitMQ.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Downstream consumers receive notifications and perform actions based on =
the job status, such as sending notifications, updating databases, or trigg=
ering other processes.</p></li>
</ol>
<p></p>
<p>Redis hashes provide an efficient way to store job details, allowing for=
 quick retrieval and updates. Unique <code>job_id</code> generation is cruc=
ial. UUID's generated by the incoming API call are preferred. Redis TTLs ar=
e used to manage data retention, ensuring that job data is automatically re=
moved after a specified period. Redis sets or sorted sets can be used to ma=
nage collections of jobs, such as active jobs or completed jobs.</p>
<p>The proposed architecture completely eliminates the need for direct HTTP=
 callbacks. All status updates and job completion events are managed within=
 the RabbitMQ and Redis ecosystem. This decoupling significantly improves s=
ystem reliability and eliminates the risk of job failures due to Notificati=
on API unavailability.</p>
<p>By removing the reliance on HTTP callbacks and utilizing RabbitMQ and Re=
dis, the Notification API can be scaled horizontally. Multiple instances of=
 the Notification API can be deployed to handle increased request loads. Th=
e Notification API does not need to maintain any local job state, as all jo=
b information is stored in Redis. This allows for easy scaling and failover=
. Furthermore, requests can be load-balanced across multiple Notification A=
PI instances.</p>
<p></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>The architecture allows for multiple downstream consumers to listen to t=
he job completion queue.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Synchronization Considerations:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Idempotency:</strong> Ensure that downstream consumers can handl=
e duplicate messages without causing unintended side effects.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Distributed Locking:</strong> If necessary, implement distribute=
d locking mechanisms (e.g., using Redis) to coordinate actions between mult=
iple consumers.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Message Ordering:</strong> If message order is critical, ensure =
that RabbitMQ delivers messages in the required order.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3.7. Downstream Consumer Design:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Downstream consumers receive notifications from the API Gateway and perf=
orm actions based on the job status.</p></li>
<li>
<p>They can perform various actions, such as sending notifications, updatin=
g databases, or triggering other processes.</p></li>
<li>
<p>These consumers are designed to be independent and scalable, allowing fo=
r flexible handling of job completion events.</p></li>
</ul>
<p></p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-AI+Common"><strong>AI+ Common</stro=
ng></h3>
<p>Because the job handling and queue management is integral to the AI+ eco=
system, it should be in a common library so that all utilities can implemen=
t it seamlessly.</p>
<p>Example utility implementation:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import re=
dis
import pika
import uuid

# Redis connection
redis_client =3D redis.Redis(host=3D'localhost', port=3D6379, db=3D0)

# RabbitMQ connection parameters
credentials =3D pika.PlainCredentials('guest', 'guest')
connection =3D pika.BlockingConnection(pika.ConnectionParameters('localhost=
', credentials=3Dcredentials))
channel =3D connection.channel()

# Declare the job status queue
channel.queue_declare(queue=3D'job_status_queue')

def process_job(media_file):
    job_id =3D str(uuid.uuid4())

    # Write initial job state to Redis
    redis_client.hset(f"job:{job_id}", mapping=3D{
        "status": "started",
        "media_file": media_file,
        "timestamp": datetime.datetime.now().isoformat()
    })

    # Publish initial status message to RabbitMQ
    channel.basic_publish(exchange=3D'', routing_key=3D'job_status_queue', =
body=3Djob_id)

    # ... (Process the AI inference job) ...

    # Update job status in Redis and publish to RabbitMQ as the job progres=
ses
    # ...

    # Example: Job completed
    redis_client.hset(f"job:{job_id}", "status", "completed")
    channel.basic_publish(exchange=3D'', routing_key=3D'job_status_queue', =
body=3Djob_id)

# Example usage
process_job("/path/to/media.mp4")
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
<p>The Notification API can implement something like the following:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">import pi=
ka
import redis

# RabbitMQ connection parameters
credentials =3D pika.PlainCredentials('guest', 'guest')
connection =3D pika.BlockingConnection(pika.ConnectionParameters('localhost=
', credentials=3Dcredentials))
channel =3D connection.channel()

# Declare the job status queue
channel.queue_declare(queue=3D'job_status_queue')

# Redis connection
redis_client =3D redis.Redis(host=3D'localhost', port=3D6379, db=3D0)

def callback(ch, method, properties, body):
    job_id =3D body.decode()

    # Read job status from Redis
    job_status =3D redis_client.hget(f"job:{job_id}", "status")

    # Send notifications based on job status
    # ...

    # Acknowledge the message
    ch.basic_ack(delivery_tag=3Dmethod.delivery_tag)

# Consume messages from the queue
channel.basic_consume(queue=3D'job_status_queue', on_message_callback=3Dcal=
lback)

print(' [*] Waiting for messages. To exit press CTRL+C')
channel.start_consuming()
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p></p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-284:AI+JobStatusManagement-Integrations">Integrations</h1>
<p>The inherent flexibility and decoupling provided by the RabbitMQ archite=
cture allows for seamless integration of additional system components, such=
 as our upcoming SOS and Ngenea integrations. By leveraging RabbitMQ as a c=
entral messaging backbone, we can establish efficient and reliable communic=
ation pathways between various services, fostering interoperability and ext=
ensibility within our ecosystem.</p>
<p>For instance, with the SOS integration, the file events generated from o=
ur Object Storage pipeline can be published to a dedicated exchange in Rabb=
itMQ. The NotificationAPI, already configured as a consumer, can subscribe =
to this exchange and receive these file events. Upon receiving an event tha=
t triggers an AI job, the NotificationAPI can parse the event data, extract=
 relevant information like the file path, and subsequently make an HTTP req=
uest to the appropriate AI utility to initiate the job. This approach maint=
ains the decoupling between components, as the SOS pipeline remains unaware=
 of the AI utilities, and the NotificationAPI acts as an intermediary, faci=
litating communication through well-defined messages.</p>
<p>Similarly, the Ngenea integration can follow a similar pattern. File eve=
nts from the NVMe storage, once published to RabbitMQ, can be consumed by t=
he NotificationAPI, which would then trigger the relevant AI utility based =
on the event data. This consistent approach to integrating new components u=
sing RabbitMQ as the messaging backbone promotes a modular and scalable arc=
hitecture. Furthermore, by utilizing RabbitMQ's features like topic-based r=
outing, we can create fine-grained subscriptions, allowing consumers to sel=
ectively receive only the events they are interested in. This enhances effi=
ciency and reduces unnecessary message processing. This extensible architec=
ture enables us to seamlessly incorporate future components and services, f=
ostering a cohesive and interconnected ecosystem driven by the robust capab=
ilities of RabbitMQ.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
